alex: FYI Android sample (retrofit + okhttp) is flaking on emulator — when token refresh occurs we get a 401 -> authenticator loop and duplicate refresh calls. Repro steps attached.

alex: Logs show repeated refreshes within ~200ms: 
```
W/OkHttp: HTTP FAILED: 401 Unauthorized
I/Auth: starting refresh token
I/Auth: refresh complete, new token=xxx
I/Auth: starting refresh token
I/Auth: refresh complete, new token=yyy
```

alex: Authenticator we shipped in sample looks like this: 
```
class TokenAuthenticator implements Authenticator {
  @Nullable
  @Override
  public Request authenticate(Route route, Response response) {
    String newToken = credentialProvider.refreshToken(); // non-blocking async in current sample
    return response.request().newBuilder()
      .header("Authorization", "Bearer " + newToken)
      .build();
  }
}
```

maria: Oof. Is this the lazy credential provider we added to avoid blocking on network calls? :face_with_monocle:

alex: Yep. The provider returns a CompletableFuture so multiple threads trigger parallel refreshes. We end up with multiple refresh calls and two slightly different tokens clobbering each other.

sam: We removed the blocking refresh helper in the Java SDK intentionally to avoid IO on OkHttp threads. But the Android sample should show a synchronized refresh pattern. Quick suggestion: use an Interceptor that blocks briefly and caches the result, or guard refreshes with a single-flight/AtomicBoolean. Example pattern: 
```
private final Object lock = new Object();
private volatile String token;
private void ensureToken() {
  if (isExpired(token)) {
    synchronized(lock) {
      if (isExpired(token)) {
        token = blockingRefresh(); // short blocking call, caches token for others
      }
    }
  }
}
// call ensureToken() from an Interceptor, not Authenticator
```

sam: Also prefer Interceptor over Authenticator on Android — Authenticator behavior varies with connection reuse on older API levels and can re-trigger requests in surprising ways.

jen: I opened a tiny PR to update the sample app and onboarding note: PR #4329. Added an Interceptor example + comment about retryOnConnectionFailure. @sam can you confirm Interceptor is the pattern we want in the official sample? :thumbsup:

sam: Confirmed — Interceptor + single-flight refresh is the recommended pattern for Android samples. We'll add a small helper class to the Java SDK to restore parity.

alex: For folks hitting this now, workaround: set `retryOnConnectionFailure(false)` in OkHttpClient and add a small jittered backoff in your refresh path.

alex: Example tweak: 
```
OkHttpClient client = new OkHttpClient.Builder()
  .retryOnConnectionFailure(false)
  .build();
```

